              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Table 4.2: Conduction of electricity by different objects or materials"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 2,
              "bbox": [
                0.205,
                0.116,
                0.879,
                0.339
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_table_2.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tr><td>S.No.</td><td>Object/Material</td><td>Observation(bulb glows/doesnot glow)</td><td>Good conductor ofelectricity or poorconductor of electricity</td></tr><tr><td>1.</td><td>Piece ofaluminium foil</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td>Iron nail</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td>Lump of sulfur(pea-sized)</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.349,
            0.883,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You might have observed that objects made of aluminium, iron, and copper make the bulb glow, whereas sulfur, coal, wood, stone, eraser, and nylon rope could not make it glow. Do you see any pattern in the glowing of bulbs when using different materials in Activity 4.4? It is observed that all the materials that make the bulb glow are metals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.462,
            0.883,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Materials that allow electricity to flow through them easily are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "good conductors of electricity",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". In contrast, materials that prevent the bulb from glowing by not allowing electricity to pass through them are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "poor conductors of electricity",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.538,
            0.884,
            0.612
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, now we can understand that the plastic covering on screwdrivers and rubber gloves protect the electrician from electric shock because these materials are poor conductors of electricity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.613,
            0.883,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We learnt that metals are generally hard, lustrous, malleable, ductile, and good conductors of heat and electricity. Now, let us learn how metals behave in the presence of air and water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.689,
            0.88,
            0.714
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4.2 Effect of Air and Water on Metals: Iron"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.733,
            0.883,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You would have often noticed that iron objects develop brown deposits when left in the open for a few days. In which conditions would an iron object develop brown deposits?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.266,
            0.791,
            0.738,
            0.811
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " When it comes into contact with dry air only."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.266,
            0.815,
            0.729,
            0.835
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " When it comes into contact with water only."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.839,
            0.803,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " When it comes into contact with both air and water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
